\begin{abstract}
Graph neural networks have become a popular choice for molecular property prediction, yet their behavior on out-of-distribution scaffolds remains poorly understood. In this work we present a systematic study of message-passing architectures across four benchmark datasets, and we show that a simple readout modification could potentially improve robustness substantially. Our best model might reach an AUC of 0.87 on the largest benchmark, which may correspond to a 12.4\% relative gain over the strongest baseline, and inference could be up to 3.2 times faster than attention-based alternatives. We further analyze failure cases and find that performance degrades sharply on molecules with rare ring systems, a limitation that practitioners should keep in mind when deploying such models in screening pipelines.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Molecular property prediction is a core task in computational drug discovery.
